## Goal of this build

Produce a complete, polished, playable game that runs entirely in a browser. Aim
for a game a person would enjoy playing: tense, readable, and fast.

{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
The build ships the whole game: the wrapping field and its star, inertial flight,
the gun and the guided torpedo, the armored splitting rocks, the escalating waves,
the hunting saucer, lives and respawns, the five screens, and the HUD. Every other
spec file states one part of it.
{{else}}
The build ships the whole game: the wrapping field and its star, inertial flight,
the gun, the splitting rocks, the escalating waves, the hunting saucer, lives and
respawns, the five screens, and the HUD. Every other spec file states one part of
it.
{{/if}}

### Hard requirements

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
- Write the runtime the game stands on: the frame loop and its delta time, the
  canvas fit, keyboard input, audio, and the debug overlay.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
- Build on the {{engine.name}} engine, taking the frame loop, the input actions,
  audio, and the debug overlay from it.
{{else}}
- Build on the {{engine.name}} engine, taking the frame loop, rendering, the input
  actions, audio, and the debug overlay from it, and writing the game inside its
  framework of game modes, actors, components, and controllers.
{{/if}}
- Render real graphics on the canvas, drawn in code. There are no art files and
  nothing is fetched at runtime.
- Run in the browser with no backend. Everything needed to play is self-contained,
  and no API key or credential is needed to build, run, or play.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
- Hold the game's whole state in `ShatterState`, as `specs/state.md` declares it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "structured-2d")}}
- Hold the game's whole authoritative state in `ShatterState`, the world's game
  state, as `specs/state.md` declares it.
{{/if}}
- Expose the debug and automation surface `specs/instrumentation.md` specifies,
  and the read-only debug overlay it describes, backed by the deterministic core
  that file requires. This is a required part of the build.
- Ship the showcase `specs/showcase.md` describes, at the root of this repository.
- Replace the starter `README.md` with one explaining what the game is, how to
  install dependencies, how to run it in development, how to produce the static
  production build, and the controls.

## Code quality

The code is fit for a long-lived codebase shared with human developers. The
type-check, lint, and format checks pass, and unit tests cover the simulation.

## Coordinate system and presentation

All positions, sizes, speeds, and ranges in this specification are given in the
game's own logical units on a fixed field of `FIELD_W x FIELD_H`
(`1280 x 720`, 16:9). The origin `(0, 0)` is the top-left; `x` increases to the
right and `y` increases downward. Angles are measured clockwise from the positive
`x` axis, in degrees unless stated otherwise, so straight up the field is `-90`
degrees.

An entity's position is its centre. Wherever the ship, a rock, a bullet, or the
saucer is given, taken, or reported as an `(x, y)` pair, that pair is the centre
of the entity rather than a corner of anything drawn for it.

- `FIELD_W x FIELD_H` is the game's logical design size. Fitting it to the browser
  window is the runtime's work: the uniform scale that preserves the aspect ratio,
  the letterboxed centring, and the device pixel ratio. The complete field is
  therefore on screen at every window size and at any pixel density, out to all
  four edges, on load and before any input.
- The letterbox bars around the field carry the field's background color.
- Draw in logical units, and take the canvas element's own size from the runtime
  alone.
- Game logic operates in logical units, independent of the rendered scale.

Every rate in this specification is per second and every duration is in seconds,
with two exceptions stated in whole simulation ticks: the gun's fire gate and the
span of a bullet's trail, both in `specs/weapons.md`.

## The screens the build has

The build carries five screens, and the game is on exactly one of them at a time:
`title`, `howto`, `playing`, `paused`, and `gameover`. `specs/ui.md` defines what
each shows and where each one leads.

## What the field shows

While the game is being played, the field carries the star at its centre, the
ship, every rock drifting on it, every shot in flight, the saucer while it is
visiting, and every saucer bullet. The HUD is drawn over the field, and the
`WAVE N` banner appears over it at the start of a wave. `specs/ui.md` gives the
readouts.

## Visual design

The field is dark. Sampled clear of every body, its background reads as deep
space: the luminance of that background is below a quarter of full.

Beyond that, choose the colors and the typography, decide how the ship, the star,
a rock, the saucer, a bullet, and the HUD are drawn, and decide what the field
looks like behind them.

Whatever look you choose, a player reads all of the following at a glance.

| What a player reads | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| The ship | The ship reads apart from the field behind it and from every other body, and its facing is legible at a glance. |
| The star | The star reads as a well: a bright core with a softer halo around it fading outward into the field. |
| The rocks | A rock reads apart from the field and from the ship, the saucer, and the bullets. |
| The saucer | The saucer reads apart from the rocks and from the ship, as a craft rather than as debris. |
| A shot in flight | A bullet reads apart from the field, and each moving bullet leaves a continuous fading tail along its recent path. |
| Thrust | While thrust is applied, a flame reads at the ship's tail, and it is absent when thrust is not. |
| Invulnerability | A ship inside its respawn grace reads as such: its drawn appearance changes visibly while the grace runs and settles once it ends. |
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
| Damage | A rock that has taken a non-destroying hit reads as damaged, more so as its health falls, and a full-health rock reads as undamaged. |
| The torpedo | The torpedo reads apart from the bullets at a glance, as the heavy weapon rather than a round. |
| The HUD | The score, the ships in reserve, and the torpedo charge are each legible at the logical field size, and nothing of the HUD is drawn over the field's centre. |
{{else}}
| The HUD | The score and the ships in reserve are each legible at the logical field size, and nothing of the HUD is drawn over the field's centre. |
{{/if}}
| Text | Every readout and every screen's text is legible against its background at the logical field size. |

The palette, the type, the glow, and every other aspect of the look are yours.

## Out of scope

- Network or online play, and any second local player. One ship is flown.
- Gamepad input.
- Persistence of scores or settings between sessions. Each session starts fresh.
- A hyperspace or teleport escape move.
